  "verbalization": "Gemtuzumab Ozogamicin is associated with Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) through multiple pathways, with the target AML subtypes being characterized by distinct genetic mutations. The evidence suggests that Gemtuzumab Ozogamicin may be effective in AML cases with Npm1 somatic mutations, Cebpa somatic mutations, or T(9;11)(p22;q23) chromosomal translocations, all of which are known to be associated with AML and may be treated with Glasdegib. Glasdegib, in turn, is indicated for AML with T(6;9)(p23;q34), a specific genetic alteration that is also linked to other AML subtypes. The overlapping indications across these pathways suggest that Gemtuzumab Ozogamicin may have a broad therapeutic potential in AML, particularly in cases where Glasdegib is a viable treatment option. The scores from the visible paths indicate a strong, consistent relationship between Gemtuzumab Ozogamicin and AML with T(6;9)(p23;q34), supported by the shared indication for Glasdegib. This integrated evidence highlights the potential for Gemtuzumab Ozogamicin to be repurposed in AML with this specific genetic profile, based on the overlapping therapeutic targets and indications across different AML subtypes."
